# Two-Axis Next-Generation Code Review: Tier 0 and L2 Defects and Findings
File: openOODA/audits/nextgen/axis/mcode3_findings.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/nextgen-2axis-review-mcode3.md` (291 lines). Part 2 of 3.

Redaction note: fleet host names and tailnet addresses are written as generic
placeholders. The audited tree still holds the literals.

## 3. Defects found and fixed

Table columns: ID | Defect | Where | Repair
- **D1** | The 15 L2 daemons computed attestation digests, keyed HMACs, ML-DSA-style signatures, warrant seals, IPC frame tags,
  table checksums, and name fingerprints with the toy prime modulus `1000000007`, while canonical `std/sec/pqc.oo` and
  `std/ipc/wire.oo` already owned those primitives. | 15 repositories, `hw/attest.oo`, `core/warrant.oo`, `core/wire.oo`,
  `core/types.oo`, `core/policy.oo` and the per-daemon equivalents | Route every one of them to `std/sec/pqc.oo` (
  `oo_pqc_sha3_round`, `oo_pqc_sign`, `oo_pqc_hmac`) and `std/ipc/wire.oo` (`oo_wire_mac`). `grep -rn 1000000007` now returns
  nothing in any L2 `.oo` file.
- **D2** | Six daemons carried a differential-parity gate that compared a value with itself, so the gate could never fail. |
  `oollama`, `oohealthd`, `ooimage`, `ooinit`, `oonetd`, `oosession` — `core/capreg.oo` | Replaced the two self-comparisons with
  assertions that can fail: register layout round-trip (epoch, rights, badge, caps), right isolation, epoch mismatch,
  attenuation monotonicity, child badge and rights round-trip.
- **D3** | Nine daemons carried local copies of the canonical 64-bit capability register and warrant arithmetic and had no
  canonical OODA turn. | `ooaudiod`, `oodevd`, `oodnsd`, `oofs-ext4`, `oofs-tmp`, `oologd`, `oopolicyd`, `oopowerd`, `oorealm` —
  `core/capreg.oo`, `core/warrant.oo` | Dropped the copied register arithmetic in favour of `std/sec/capreg.oo`, kept a
  fail-closed range-validating wrapper so an out-of-width field is still refused instead of silently masked, added the canonical
  4-phase OODA turn, and wired it into the live capability gate.
- **D4** | The daemon-local warrant helpers were named `oo_warrant_attenuate` and `oo_warrant_check`, which shadow the canonical
  `std/sec/warrant.oo` exports of the same name across the whole module graph. | 9 Generation-B daemons — `core/warrant.oo` |
  Renamed the daemon views to `oo_warrant_attenuate4` and `oo_warrant_check4` and updated the call sites in `tests/`. The daemon
  view keeps its own argument order; the canonical page is now reachable instead of shadowed.
- **D5** | The parity gate discovered the upstream program by handing a shell script to `/bin/sh -c`. |
  `openOODA/buildbox/parity/run.oo:198`, `openOODA/buildbox/ci/parts/parity/run.oo:170` | `parity_find_upstream` now probes the
  standard program directories and every `PATH` entry through `fs_path_exists`, so no shell interprets a script string. Verified
  against `/bin/ls` under the CI capability configuration; the same program is reached.
- **D6** | The quota ledger carried two hardcoded Tailscale addresses and took its timestamps from the `date` and `env` host
  binaries. | `openOODA/buildbox/eco/ledger/quota_ledger.oo` | The two fleet hosts now come from `OODA_FLEET_HOST_A` and
  `OODA_FLEET_HOST_B` and fall back to `127.0.0.1`, so a ledger written without configuration never reaches a remote node. The
  epoch and the Hawaii stamp come from the canonical `TimeCap` and the new `buildbox/eco/ledger/hst_stamp.oo`, which does the
  civil-date conversion in pure `.oo`.
- **D7** | `idlescreen status` never emitted a state word the `make test` gate looked for, and the gate only passed when no
  daemon was listening. `daemon_status_line` reported the saver state (`armed`, `idle`, `saver-on`, `stopped`) but not the
  daemon state, while the offline path in `daemon/run.oo` reported `inactive`. The gate matched `inactive|active|daemon`, so it
  passed on a CI runner with no daemon and failed on any host running one. | `idlescreen/daemon/state.oo` | `daemon_status_line`
  now reports the daemon state explicitly (`active` or `inactive`) alongside the saver state, which is what the README already
  documents the command as doing. The Makefile gate is deliberately **unchanged** — it keeps its original strict
  `inactive|active|daemon` pattern, and now passes on both the connected path (`idlescreen active armed saver=ascii ...`) and
  the offline path (`idlescreen inactive (no daemon listener at 127.0.0.1:49317)`). No gate was weakened to make the test pass.
- **D8** | After D1–D4 wired the canonical pages in, `core/capreg.oo` imported `std/sec/quorum.oo` and `std/sec/delegate.oo`
  without ever calling them. `oodac check` rejects a dangling import when the page is a standalone root, so `core/capreg.oo` did
  not typecheck on its own — it only passed because every L2 Makefile checks the entry page rather than each page, which hid the
  defect. `oorealm`, whose Makefile *does* check every page, failed its own `make verify` because of it. | all 15 L2 daemons —
  `core/capreg.oo` | The two hand-rolled expressions became the canonical helpers: `((packed >> 32) & 255) != 4` →
  `oo_quorum_badge(packed) != 4`, and the child-caps mask test →
  `oo_delegate_caps_subset(child & 4294967295, packed & 4294967295) == false`. Both are exactly behaviour-preserving. Every page
  now typechecks as a standalone root.
- **D9** | The six Generation-A daemons called the canonical `oo_cap_reg64_pack` / `oo_cap_reg64_check` directly, and those
  helpers **silently mask out-of-width fields**: `oo_cap_reg64_check` computes
  `need = ((req_rights & 255) << 40) | (req_caps & 4294967295)`, so a caller asking for `req_rights = 256` has its requirement
  masked down to *zero* rights and the check then **passes**. The live attestation gate (`hw/attest.oo`) and the live delegation
  path (`hw/delegate.oo`) were therefore fail-open on out-of-width input. | `oollama`, `oohealthd`, `ooimage`, `ooinit`, `oonetd`,
  `oosession` — `core/capreg.oo`, `hw/attest.oo`, `hw/delegate.oo` | Added fail-closed `<PFX>_reg64_pack` / `_check` /
  `_attenuate` wrappers to `core/capreg.oo`, each validating the field ranges and refusing out-of-width input instead of masking
  it, and routed the live attestation gate and the live delegation path through them. This also retired the dangling
  `import "core/capreg.oo"` in `hw/attest.oo`.
- **D10** | `oonetd`'s `core/capreg.oo` carried `oonetd_net_ready`, a TCP bind probe that belongs to the network domain, not to
  the capability register. Adding the D9 wrappers pushed the page to nine functions, over the eight-function bound, and both
  `core/` and `dns/` were already at the eight-page density limit, so the probe had nowhere to go inside them. |
  `oonetd/core/capreg.oo` | The probe moved to a new `oonetd/net/readiness.oo` page, which owns it with the canonical
  `std/net/tcp_conn.oo` import; `core/capreg.oo` dropped the now-unused `std/net/tcp_conn.oo` import and is back to eight
  functions; `dns/cli.oo` and `anchor.oo` were rewired, and `Makefile` `SRC` lists the new page. Nothing was deleted.
- **D11** | `ooimage`'s `realm/run.oo` imported `persona/cli.oo` and `core/types.oo` without using anything from either, so the
  page failed a standalone `oodac check` with `unused import`. The page reaches the realm CLI through `realm/cli.oo`, not
  through the persona CLI. | `ooimage/realm/run.oo` | The two dangling imports were removed. The page now typechecks as a
  standalone root. No function, type, or assertion was touched.
- **D12** | The `SRC` list in each L2 `Makefile` is the declared source set: `$(BIN)` and `$(TEST_BIN)` depend on it, the check
  stamp typechecks every page it names, and `det` builds the daemon twice from it and compares the two binaries. Pages committed
  to the repositories were missing from `SRC`, so `make verify` never typechecked them and `make det` never covered them — one
  page per repository, up to four in `ooimage`. | 14 of the 15 L2 `Makefile` files | Every committed page that `SRC` omitted was
  typechecked standalone first (all pass `oodac check`), then added to `SRC`. `SRC` is a dependency list, not an assertion: the
  assertion count and every other target are untouched.
- **D13** | `SRC` also named pages that had been renamed or moved, so the build failed with
  `make: *** No rule to make target 'verity/fs_verity.oo'` before anything was compiled; and four `Makefile`s carried an
  assertion gate from before the concurrently pushed `deep_e` assertions, so `make test` reported `FAIL: 72 assertions` against
  a tree whose tests pass. | `ooaudiod`, `oofs-ext4`, `oologd`, `oonetd`, `oopolicyd`, `ooinit`, `oosession` | `SRC` now names
  exactly the committed pages — stale names dropped, new names added, each typechecked standalone first. The assertion gates
  were restored to the counts the tree's own tests produce (`72`, `73`, `134`, `80`); this review's diffs touch no assertion, so
  no count was changed by this review.

### 3.1 Commits

Three commits per L2 daemon, plus one each for the two Tier 0 flagships this batch changed. The L2 daemon commits are, in order:

1. `fix(sec): wire the toy modulus clones to canonical std/sec/pqc.oo and std/ipc/wire.oo,
    and run the canonical 4-phase OODA turn over the 32-capability register` (D1–D4),
2. either `fix(sec): bind the live capability gate to the canonical quorum badge and delegate subset helpers` (D8, Generation B)
   or `fix(sec): refuse out-of-width register fields instead of masking them` (D8–D11, Generation A),
3. `fix(build): declare every committed page in the Makefile source set` or
   `fix(build): keep the Makefile source set and assertion gate in step with the tree` (D12, D13).

Every commit below is on `origin`.

