## 4. Findings reported, not fixed

### F1 — Canonical L0 `std/sec/speculate.oo` did not compile, and broke every consumer of the canonical OODA engine

On entry, `std/sec/speculate.oo:21` called `oo_arena_mark()`, a symbol that is defined nowhere in `std`, in `std-hw`, or in the
compiler's builtin set. The canonical `std/core/ooda.oo` imports `std/sec/speculate.oo`, so **the canonical OODA engine did not
typecheck**, and every daemon importing it failed:

```
ERR	type	Type error at 21:41: undefined variable 'oo_arena_mark'
--> /Projects/openOODA-core/std/sec/speculate.oo:21:41
let mark : Int = if arena_mark < 0 { oo_arena_mark ( ) } else { arena_mark } ;
```

Introduced by `std` commit `5560098` (2026-11-05 12:53 -1000, "wire real arena mark/reset"). It broke `oollama`, `ooflash`, and
the six L2 daemons that import `std/core/ooda.oo`.

*Status.* `std` is claimed by `w3:p1` and its owner had the fix in flight during this review: the new canonical page
`std/core/arena_mark.oo` appeared in the working tree at 17:56 HST with `oo_arena_mark_zero`, `oo_arena_mark_valid`, and
`oo_arena_reset_mark(mark, cap)`, and `std/sec/speculate.oo` was rewired to it. The canonical engine typechecked again from
18:00 HST, and this batch was verified against that state. **`std` was deliberately not modified by this review** — it is
outside this batch's claim.

*Remediation.* Land the `std` owner's `core/arena_mark.oo` change, then re-run `make verify` and `make test` in every consumer
of `std/core/ooda.oo`.

### F2 — The ecosystem has no independent oracle for the capability register

Section 2.4 records that `std-hw` commit `90b8cd1` collapsed `rt_cap_reg64_*` into aliases of the canonical `oo_cap_reg64_*`. A
differential-parity gate needs two independent implementations; after that commit there is one at the `.oo` level, so any `rt_*`
against `oo_*` comparison is vacuous.

*Remediation.* Either restore an independent implementation in `std-hw/hw/runtime/rt_caps_reg64.oo` — the pre-`90b8cd1` body is
a correct second implementation of the documented layout — or compare the `.oo` register against the C runtime in
`oodar/sec/cap/caps.h` through the differential harness, which is the only oracle that is genuinely independent.

### F3 — `idlescreen` reaches the host through an implicit shell

`idlescreen/core/shell.oo` exposes `shell_out(proc, cmd)`, which runs a single command **string** through `process_exec`, and
`idlescreen/cli/service_unit.oo` uses it for filesystem probes:

```
    if shell_out(proc, "test -x /usr/local/bin/idlescreen 2>/dev/null && echo yes") != "" {
        return "/usr/local/bin/idlescreen";
    }
    if shell_out(proc, "test -x /usr/bin/idlescreen 2>/dev/null && echo yes") != "" { return "/usr/bin/idlescreen"; }
```

This is the `/bin/sh -c` shape the house law forbids, and it hardcodes two system paths.

*Remediation.* Give `service_bin_path` and `service_has_systemd` an `&FsReadCap` and probe with `fs_path_exists` instead of
`test -x`. The call sites already have a read capability available.

### F4 — `ooflash` shells out to `powershell -Command` for the Windows path helpers

`ooflash/flash/winpath.oo` and `ooflash/flash/digest.oo` run `sys_exec(sys, "powershell", "-NoProfile", "-Command", q)`. The
argv is explicit and the host fails closed off Windows, which is much better than a bare shell string, but `-Command` still
hands a script to an interpreter.

*Remediation.* Replace the one-liners with direct Win32 calls, or restrict them to an explicit `ProcessCap` command allow-list
so the script text is never interpreted.
